WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.800 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:05.938 line:0%
SAM LEVINSON:
<i> I always write to music
and as I was writing</i> Euphoria,

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:08.007 line:0%
I kept hearing Danny Elfman...

00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:10.143 line:0%
♪ (CURIOUS MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.178 line:0%
-...meets Yeezus...
-♪ (ELECTRONIC BEATS) ♪

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:13.479 line:0%
...meets gospel...

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.748 line:0%
♪ (GOSPEL TONES) ♪

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:16.983 line:0%
...which sounds really weird.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.485 line:0%
♪ (COMBINATION
OF MUSIC STYLES) ♪

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:22.188 line:0%
Labrinth is the first person
who's like, "Yeah."

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.590 line:0%
♪ ("EUPHORIA" THEME PLAYS) ♪

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.960 line:0%
I felt like I knew exactly
what he wanted to go for.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.062 line:0%
The wonderful thing about Lab
is he works from

00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:31.764 line:0%
<i> just pure inspiration.</i>

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.601 line:0%
Most score writers, they have
a very traditional way

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.168 line:0%
<i> of writing.
 They've gone to school,</i>

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:39.372 line:0%
so they're used to
a certain sound, and for me,

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.308 line:0%
I feel like<i> Euphoria</i>
told me what to do.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:45.812 line:0%
Whatever makes me
feel excited,

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:48.614 line:0%
I'm gonna find a way
of injecting it into the music.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:51.117 line:0%
♪ (THEME CONTINUES) ♪

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.419 line:0%
♪<i> Feel the morning ♪</i>

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.254 line:0%
<i> ♪ On my face ♪</i>

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:57.423 line:0%
For me, with every scene,
it was just like,

00:00:57.556 --> 00:01:00.126 line:0%
"How can I elevate it
but do it in a modern,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.327 line:0%
<i> but fresh way?"</i>

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:04.330 line:0%
Labrinth would bring out
certain hues in the show.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.800 line:0%
LABRINTH:<i> I wanted to merge
 different genres,</i>

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.503 line:0%
and a lot of my music
has influences

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:12.104 line:0%
of classic '70s rock,

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:14.540 line:0%
but also has orchestral
arrangements,

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:15.608 line:0%
along with hip-hop.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.878 line:0%
<i> I kinda have two styles,</i>

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.213 line:0%
maybe my angel
and my devil side.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:23.416 line:0%
♪ (ETHEREAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.289 line:0%
LABRINTH:
<i>I'm a very spontaneous creative.</i>

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:33.793 line:0%
One day, I wanna make
a house record...

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:36.329 line:0%
♪ (DANCE MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.397 line:0%
...the next day, I wanna make
a classical record...

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.867 line:0%
♪ (ORCHESTRAL MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.669 line:0%
...the next day, I wanna make
an electronic record.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:46.106 line:0%
♪ (ELECTRONIC MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.644 line:0%
I'm not a precious person.
When I write music,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.479 line:0%
I'm like, "Let's all get
involved together."

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.549 line:0%
Every collaboration helps
develop my music.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.319 line:0%
♪ ("EUPHORIA" THEME PLAYS) ♪

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.590 line:0%
LEVINSON:
<i>There's nothing we love more
 than to just sit there</i>

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:06.025 line:0%
and cut Lab's music.
And just play around with it.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.228 line:0%
<i> We'll play it backwards,
 we'll strip it up,</i>

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:10.896 line:0%
<i>and just try different things.</i>

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.132 line:0%
<i> So, it becomes this, uh,
 this good, sort of,</i>

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:14.267 line:0%
you know, back and forth.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.404 line:0%
JULIO:<i> It's a very, very unique
relationship with a composer</i>

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.406 line:0%
that's, uh, I don't know,
led to, I think,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.441 line:0%
a very one-of-a-kind texture.

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:26.112 line:0%
♪ (THEME CONTINUES) ♪

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.147 line:0%
<i>♪ I'm livin' for the thrill ♪</i>

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:31.818 line:0%
LABRINTH:<i> Whatever the score
needed, I would adjust my style.</i>

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.553 line:0%
<i>Euphoria</i> gave me the freedom

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.156 line:0%
to be whatever artist
I wanna be.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:40.359 line:0%
There's this, kind of,
hopefulness to his music

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:43.930 line:0%
<i> that feels triumphant
 and serves as, kind of,</i>

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.865 line:0%
<i>a north star for the series
 itself.</i>

00:02:47.332 --> 00:02:49.068 line:0%
Labrinth's score was just...

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.538 line:0%
better than I could
ever dream of.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.274 line:0%
♪ ( THEME CONTINUES) ♪

